WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.580
Open Rack evolved as operators sought shared interfaces and more flexible infrastructure.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:08.400
The useful history is the sequence of constraints, not a list of

3
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:08.760
dates.

4
00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.920
A shared mechanical arrangement helps equipment integrate.

5
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.280
Rack-level power distribution changes where conversion and distribution hardware sit.

6
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.880
Increasing density makes electrical, thermal and service boundaries more demanding.

7
00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:24.760
Open Rack version three, or O R V three, is our main

8
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:25.460
starting point.

9
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.840
High-power rack implementations extend the power architecture for heavier loads.

10
00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:34.440
Open Rack Wide, or O R W, expands the mechanical envelope to

11
00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.140
support different equipment packaging and integration needs.

12
00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:41.960
These are related developments, not interchangeable names for one rack with ever-larger

13
00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:42.400
ratings.

14
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:46.560
The animation builds this progression around the constraint each step addresses.

15
00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:51.160
Notice that changing the frame doesn't automatically validate its electrical system, and

16
00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:53.540
adding power doesn't establish cooling capacity.

17
00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:57.440
We will use version and implementation names precisely throughout the course.

18
00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:01.780
When a claim includes dimensions, mass or power, the next question is

19
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:05.280
which document, configuration and revision make that claim true.

